# Daylily Ephemeral Cluster

DYEC is the command-line control plane for short-lived AWS ParallelCluster
bioinformatics environments. This concise guide describes the `18.0.57` release.
Start with [docs/agent_cli_guide.md](docs/agent_cli_guide.md) for the common
operator/agent path, then use [README.md](README.md) and
[docs/cli_reference.md](docs/cli_reference.md) for full detail.

DYEC creates and inspects clusters, opens a supported SSM headnode shell,
mounts explicit S3 run prefixes into FSx, launches explicitly pinned DayOA
commands, records analysis-root activity, and exports completed analysis roots
through a temporary FSx DRA. It is provider-neutral: it uses supplied local
configuration, manifests, and S3 paths rather than contacting an identity or
metadata service.

## Start safely

```bash
cd /path/to/daylily-ephemeral-cluster
source ./activate

export AWS_PROFILE=<named-profile>
export REGION=us-west-2
export REGION_AZ=us-west-2d
export CLUSTER=<cluster-name>
export ANALYSIS_ID=<analysis-id>
export EXECUTING_ENTITY=<executing-entity>
export DAY_EX_CFG="$HOME/.config/daylily/daylily_ephemeral_cluster.yaml"

dyec --json version
dyec --help
dyec agent guidance
```

`dyec` and `daylily-ec` are installed entrypoints for the same CLI. Current
operator docs use `dyec`.

## Optional project-local context

For repeated DYEC invocations from one checkout, store only the four supported
values in the ignored file `$PWD/.dyec.config.yaml`:

```bash
dyec set-vars \
  --profile "$AWS_PROFILE" \
  --region "$REGION" \
  --region-az "$REGION_AZ" \
  --cluster-admin-email operator@example.org

dyec -v --json info
```

For `--profile`, `--region`, and `--region-az`, the order is explicit flag,
local context, then the command's established behavior. The file is local to
the current working directory, accepts only the four documented string keys,
and never reads or writes `DYEC_*` environment variables. Use
`dyec unset-vars --region` to clear one key or `dyec unset-vars` to clear all
keys and remove an empty file.

## Cluster lifecycle

```bash
dyec preflight \
  --profile "$AWS_PROFILE" \
  --region-az "$REGION_AZ" \
  --config "$DAY_EX_CFG"

dyec create \
  --profile "$AWS_PROFILE" \
  --region-az "$REGION_AZ" \
  --config "$DAY_EX_CFG"

dyec --json cluster list --profile "$AWS_PROFILE" --region "$REGION"
dyec headnode connect --profile "$AWS_PROFILE" --region "$REGION" --cluster "$CLUSTER"
```

For one create, `--admin-email` overrides the AWS Budget notification recipient.
Otherwise the create YAML's `budget_email` takes precedence over the local
`cluster_admin_email`; heartbeat email handling is unchanged.

## Catalog-first DayOA launch

The shipped catalog targets DayOA `15.0.37`. Inspect a command before launch;
its `input_contract` determines whether it needs a local six-manifest directory,
a staged sample-manifest input, a run-context TSV, or no input files.

```bash
dyec --json catalog list --type prod
dyec --json catalog show <command-id>

# Example for a six-manifest command.
dyec --json catalog render <command-id> \
  --analysis-id "$ANALYSIS_ID" \
  --executing-entity "$CLUSTER" \
  --profile "$AWS_PROFILE" \
  --region "$REGION" \
  --cluster "$CLUSTER" \
  --manifest-dir ./config \
  --payload-staging-s3-uri s3://<bucket>/<temporary-relay-prefix>/ \
  --dry-run
```

`validation_pending: true` means a command's current `git_tag` differs from
its retained `validated_version`. It is visibility only: it does not rewrite old
receipts or block a launch.

For a non-catalog launch, provide the DayOA ref explicitly. DYEC does not infer
or default it:

```bash
dyec workflow launch \
  --profile "$AWS_PROFILE" \
  --region "$REGION" \
  --cluster "$CLUSTER" \
  --analysis-id "$ANALYSIS_ID" \
  --executing-entity "$CLUSTER" \
  --git-tag 15.0.37 \
  --manifest-dir ./config \
  --payload-staging-s3-uri s3://<bucket>/<temporary-relay-prefix>/ \
  --dy-command "dy-r <exact-target> -p -k -j 6"
```

## Export and deletion

Record the visit before exporting a completed analysis root. The default export
preserves FSx data; deletion is a separate destructive operation.

```bash
dyec analysis visit \
  --analysis-root "/fsx/analysis_results/$CLUSTER/$ANALYSIS_ID" \
  --mode export \
  --intent "export completed analysis results without FSx deletion"

dyec export \
  --profile "$AWS_PROFILE" \
  --region "$REGION" \
  --cluster "$CLUSTER" \
  --source-path "/fsx/analysis_results/$CLUSTER/$ANALYSIS_ID" \
  --destination-s3-uri "s3://<results-bucket>/<prefix>/$CLUSTER/$ANALYSIS_ID/" \
  --output-dir "./export-receipts/$ANALYSIS_ID" \
  --wait --timeout-seconds 5400

dyec delete --dry-run --profile "$AWS_PROFILE" --region "$REGION" --cluster "$CLUSTER"
```

Keep the intended destination empty before export; do not use `--s3-visit-uri`
for that prefix because the marker would defeat the empty-prefix preflight.
Keep `fsx_export.yaml` and verify `status: success`, `task_lifecycle:
SUCCEEDED`, and `detached: true` before considering deletion. Run a live delete
only after separate approval for that exact cluster.

